{{/*
  grid-navigator — the cell system in play, the cell held, and the subgrid.

  What a cell system is and what a cell draws belong to the analysis lane
  (issue #5 §5.4); which one is chosen and which cell is held belong to the
  session. This region is the second half only, which is why it names a system
  and never computes one.
*/}}
{{define "grid-navigator"}}<div id="atlas-grid-navigator" class="grid-navigator"{{if not .Grid.Enabled}} hidden{{end}}>
  {{/*
    One small button wearing the current system's mark, cycling to the next on
    click -- and nothing at all when geohash is the only system this world
    offers. The full name lives on the field label beside it.
  */}}
  <button type="button" class="grid-system" id="grid-system"{{if not .Grid.Cycles}} hidden{{end}}
          title="Dividing by {{.Grid.Name}} · ⌘G for {{.Grid.Next}}"
          aria-label="Cell system: {{.Grid.Name}}. Switch to {{.Grid.Next}}"
          hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"system":"cycle"}'>{{.Grid.Mark}}</button>
  {{/*
    Escape and the arrow telescope out one level. At the root there is no
    level left, so the same press closes the grid -- which is what the title
    says rather than leaving a reader to find out.
  */}}
  <button type="button" id="grid-back" aria-label="Back one cell level"
          title="{{if .Grid.Cell}}Back one {{.Grid.System}} level{{else}}Close {{.Grid.System}} grid{{end}}"
          hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"cell":"{{.Grid.Cell}}","ascend":"1"}'>←</button>
  {{/*
    NO `changed` ON THIS FIELD, and the reason is that the server writes into
    it. `changed` remembers the value the last event carried and refuses one
    that matches -- which is right for a search box nobody else touches, and
    wrong here: the field is re-rendered by every swap, so ascending out of
    "m" leaves an empty field beside a remembered "m", and a reader who types
    the same hash again is answered with nothing at all. The event this hangs
    on is `input`, which a browser raises only when the value actually moved
    under the reader's hands, so the modifier was buying nothing to begin
    with. The reference had no such memory either: one input listener, one
    address, every time (`frontend/src/events.js`).
  */}}
  <label>
    <span id="grid-system-name">{{.Grid.Name}}</span>
    <input type="text" id="grid-input" name="cell" value="{{.Grid.Cell}}"{{if .Grid.Length}} maxlength="{{.Grid.Length}}"{{end}}
           inputmode="text" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="none" spellcheck="false"
           aria-label="Grid cell"
           hx-post="/session/grid" hx-trigger="input delay:150ms">
  </label>
  <button type="button" class="subgrid-toggle" id="subgrid-toggle"
          aria-pressed="{{.Grid.Subgrid}}"
          aria-label="{{if .Grid.Subgrid}}Hide the subgrid{{else}}Show the subgrid{{end}}"
          title="Space · show or hide the subgrid"
          hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"subgrid":"{{if .Grid.Subgrid}}0{{else}}1{{end}}"}'>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
      <path d="M2.5 2.5h11v11h-11z"/>
      <path d="M6.2 2.5v11M9.8 2.5v11M2.5 6.2h11M2.5 9.8h11"/>
      <path class="subgrid-toggle-slash" d="m2.6 13.4 10.8-10.8"/>
    </svg>
  </button>
</div>
{{end}}
